    "created_at": "2024-12-16T15:11:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-12-16T15:13:13Z",
    "body": "FWIW I disagree with a lot of the points being made in that section and say that (HttpOnly) Cookies are the right place to store session identifiers in most cases, both for security and performance reasons. This article seems to operate under the assumption that all applications exclusively use JavaScript for rendering and fetching information, and that credential theft doesn't exist. Many web applications make the sound decision to rely on server-side rendering and use cookies to obtain session credentials as part of those requests. A better title for this section may have been \"Don't use cookies as an arbitrary key-value store in your application\", and I think the web has decent alternatives for that:\r\n\r\n- Server-side storage with a cookie session identifier\r\n- LocalStorage / SessionStorage for easy storage of small amounts of data\r\n- IndexedDB for more sophisticated storage needs",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
